{
  "version": 1,
  "comments": {
    "c1": {
      "anchor": "t32",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "A first-word index for big rule groups",
      "excerpt": "A first-word index for big rule groups",
      "text": "Do it.  In fact, if there are ANY (small set of) constant words in the sequence you should be able to do an immediate elimination of the rule by just scanning the line.  What would this do to speed?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-04T02:00:21.071Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-04T02:01:45.886Z",
          "html": "<p>Yes, doing it.  Your version is better than the first-word index:  each line's words go into a set ONCE, and a rule whose syntax has words it can't do without (<code>set ... to</code>, <code>is a menu of</code>, <code>starting at</code>) is skipped at once when any of them is missing from the line.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Safe:  only words outside optional parts count, so it can only skip a rule that could never match.</li>\n  <li>The catch:  most rules write their own <code>parse()</code>, so each one says which words it needs (or the check reads them off its syntax);  a rule that says nothing is still tried.</li>\n  <li>What it does to speed:  not known until measured.  P21 counted 2.3 million rule tries on the tests, most turned away by their quick test;  this turns most of those away for the cost of a set lookup.  The build reports it with <code>speedtest --by-module</code>, before and after.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Queued after P24, before P26:  the build agent is on P23 and P24 now.</p>"
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "at": "2026-11-04T02:03:30.588Z",
          "text": "\"most rules write their own parse\" -- get them from the constant words in the syntax.\nBTW: I suspect that \"first starting literal strings\" will still save something since you won't have to look through the sequence at all, just hard-stop immediately.\n\nFor things like \"is a menu of\", I assume you'll\n- look across the list of tokens for \"is\", if found\n- check if the next token is \"menu\", if found\n- check if the next token us \"of\"...\n- etc?\n\nWould keep track of the fact that you found the \"is\" at the start?  Does that add anything other than complexity?",
          "edited": "2026-11-04T02:06:02.211Z"
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-04T02:06:57.449Z",
          "html": "<p>Agreed on both:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><b>From the syntax:</b>  every rule's required words are read off its syntax, even when it writes its own <code>parse()</code>:  nearly all of those parse their syntax first and only turn more away after.  The few that don't read their syntax at all say so and are always tried.</li>\n  <li><b>The first word still counts:</b>  P22's first-word check stays in front, a hard stop before anything else.  The set check catches the words later in the line.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>\"is a menu of\":</b>  no walking the tokens.  The line's words go into a set once;  the check is three lookups:  is <code>is</code> in it, is <code>menu</code>, is <code>of</code>.  No order, no positions.  Only a rule that passes is parsed for real, and that parse finds them in order.</p>\n<p><b>Remembering where \"is\" was found:</b>  not worth it.  The real parse has to place each part itself (what comes before <code>is</code> decides where it lands, and <code>is</code> can be in a line more than once), so a remembered position saves nothing it would use, and adds bookkeeping to every line.  The set says \"can't match\" cheaply;  the parse says \"does match\" exactly.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
